Please review the following REST API design for a model-serving platform and provide detailed feedback. Identify any violations of REST conventions, HTTP semantics, or API usability best practices. For each issue found, explain the problem and propose a corrected design.

Proposed API:

POST /api/v1/model/load
Body: { "model_path": "/models/gemma-4-e2b-it-mxfp8", "kv_quant": "k8v4", "force": true }
Response 200: { "status": "loaded", "model_id": "gemma-4-e2b-it-mxfp8", "load_time_ms": 4832 }
Response 200: { "status": "already_loaded", "model_id": "gemma-4-e2b-it-mxfp8" }
Notes: Returns 200 in both success and already-loaded cases.

GET /api/v1/model/status?model_id=gemma-4-e2b-it-mxfp8
Response 200: { "loaded": true, "kv_quant": "k8v4", "slots_used": 3 }
Response 200: { "loaded": false }
Notes: Returns 200 even when the model is not found.

POST /api/v1/infer
Body: { "model_id": "gemma-4-e2b-it-mxfp8", "prompt": "What is the capital of France?", "max_tokens": 128, "temperature": 0.7, "stream": false }
Response 200: { "output": "The capital of France is Paris.", "tokens": 8, "tps": 72.3 }
Response 200: { "error": "model_not_loaded" }
Notes: Errors are returned with HTTP 200 and an error field in the body.

DELETE /api/v1/model/unload?model_id=gemma-4-e2b-it-mxfp8
Notes: Uses DELETE with a query parameter instead of path parameter.
Response 200: { "status": "unloaded" }
Response 200: { "status": "not_loaded" }

POST /api/v1/model/reload
Body: { "model_id": "gemma-4-e2b-it-mxfp8", "kv_quant": "k8v8" }
Notes: Separate endpoint for reload; triggers unload + load.
Response 200: { "status": "reloaded", "load_time_ms": 5100 }

GET /api/v1/metrics
Notes: Returns plain text Prometheus format.

POST /api/v1/cache/flush
Body: { "model_id": "gemma-4-e2b-it-mxfp8", "scope": "ram" }
Notes: Flushes the RAM prompt-cache for the model. No authentication check.

Please evaluate: HTTP method correctness, status code usage, error handling conventions, resource naming and URL structure, idempotency guarantees, and any security concerns. Then propose a clean revised API that corrects the identified issues, following OpenAPI 3.1 conventions. Include the revised endpoint definitions with their HTTP methods, paths, request/response shapes, and status codes.
